<Article slug="invalidation">
<p>The shop changes a mug’s price from £12 to £15. The application commits the new price to its database, then deletes the old product card from Redis. Both operations succeed.</p>
<p>A moment later, Redis contains £12 again.</p>
<p>No command has failed. Another request had already read the old price and was still preparing its cache fill. Deleting a saved answer does not delete the copies held by work in progress.</p>
<p>Below, advance the reader twice, advance the writer twice, then let the reader finish. Watch the price in the reader’s hand as well as the price in the database.</p>
<InvalidationRace />
<p>The reader’s first step misses. Its second step obtains £12. The writer commits £15 and deletes the cache entry. Then the reader saves the £12 it has been holding. A later visitor can now hit that old value until another repair removes it.</p>

<h2>Invalidate after the source transaction commits</h2>
<p><strong>Cache invalidation</strong> stops an old saved answer being reused. Deletion is one implementation; changing a generation, marking a value invalid or moving readers to another version can also do it. The important event is when old work ceases to be eligible to answer new requests.</p>
<p>For ordinary cache-aside, update the source first and invalidate after the update has committed. A database <strong>commit</strong> makes the transaction’s changes visible according to that database’s transaction rules. Sending a cache notification before a transaction commits can advertise a change that is not yet readable, or one that later rolls back.</p>
<p>Choose the early-delete schedule above. Deletion makes the cache miss while the source still says £12. A reader loads and saves £12; only afterwards does the writer commit £15. There is no later deletion to remove the newly saved old value. Moving invalidation after commit closes this particular window.</p>
<p>It does not close the stale-refill schedule in the opening. Use Back to inspect where the old value was obtained: before the commit, even though it was saved afterwards. This is why a successful <code>DEL</code> reply is evidence about one Redis operation, not proof that future readers will see the new source state.</p>
<p>Read-your-writes code also needs to respect the source’s visibility rules. For example, PostgreSQL’s default Read Committed isolation gives a plain query a snapshot from when that query began. A source query already in progress need not see a concurrent commit. Other isolation levels and replica reads can retain older views for different reasons.</p>

<h2>Say which consistency guarantee the application needs</h2>
<p>“Keep the cache consistent” leaves several different requirements unresolved. Consider the shop’s editor and a public visitor:</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>What would violate it?</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Read your writes</strong>: after the editor successfully changes the price, their next view includes that change.</td><td>The save succeeds, but the editor immediately sees £12 again.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Bounded staleness</strong>: a committed price change becomes visible within an agreed interval.</td><td>Visitors can still receive the old price after that interval.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Eventual consistency</strong>: after changes stop and repair work succeeds, readers converge on the final state.</td><td>A lost invalidation leaves an old entry reusable indefinitely.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The interval matters. Eventual convergence alone does not specify a deadline. A TTL helps repair forgotten entries, but its bound depends on the source age, loading delay and renewal behaviour from the previous article. Continuous writes may also mean there is no single final value to converge on yet.</p>
<p>For the editor’s immediate response, the application can return the committed result directly. For a subsequent read, it can use an authoritative path or carry a minimum source revision that the read must satisfy. A fixed “bypass the cache for one second” rule only establishes a guarantee if the relevant delays really fit that second.</p>
<p>A purchase is a different operation again. The display cache can help draw the page, while the purchase transaction revalidates the price and availability against authoritative state. Choosing a cache policy should not quietly move a business decision onto a copy with weaker guarantees.</p>
<p>The mechanisms below address two specific problems: old loaders filling after invalidation, and invalidations being lost between systems. Neither provides every consistency guarantee in the table by itself.</p>

<h2>Reject stale refills with a generation check</h2>
<p>The old reader needs a way to discover that its permission to fill the cache has been revoked. Give this product’s cache a <strong>generation token</strong>, initially <code>g1</code>. A loader captures that token before reading the source.</p>
<p>After a source commit, invalidation changes the token to a new value, <code>g2</code>, and deletes the saved answer. When the old reader tries to fill, it must compare its captured <code>g1</code> with the current token. They differ, so the fill is rejected.</p>
<InvalidationRace guarded />
<p>Load the stale-refill schedule. The source is £15, the reader still holds £12, but Redis stays empty. A new reader can load the current source rather than inheriting this reader’s obsolete snapshot. Load the serial schedule to see an ordinary fill succeed.</p>
<p>The comparison and the write must be one <strong>atomic</strong> Redis operation: no other Redis command may slip between them. A separate <code>GET generation</code>, application comparison and <code>SET value</code> recreates the race. Invalidation could occur after the comparison but before the fill.</p>
<p>A short Lua script can perform the conditional fill on Redis. Another script rotates the token and deletes the value together. Here is the essential fill operation; the <code>KEYS</code> and <code>ARGV</code> arrays are the key names and arguments supplied by the caller:</p>
<pre><code>{`-- KEYS[1]: generation key; KEYS[2]: value key
-- ARGV[1]: captured token; ARGV[2]: encoded value; ARGV[3]: TTL
if redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]) ~= ARGV[1] then
  return 0
end
redis.call('SET', KEYS[2], ARGV[2], 'EX', ARGV[3])
return 1`}</code></pre>
<p>A zero result means “do not save this fill”, not “the product does not exist”. The request may still return the source result it already read if that is allowed by its contract. If it needs a fresher result, use a bounded retry or an authoritative path; repeatedly racing to refill is not a correctness strategy.</p>
<p>This token is not the value’s schema version, nor is it the product’s data revision. It identifies an invalidation boundary. Capture it <em>before</em> the source read. Capturing it afterwards could attach a new token to an old result and incorrectly authorise the fill. The source view must also be current relative to that capture: querying a lagging replica or reusing an older transaction snapshot can attach stale data to the new token.</p>

<h2>Metadata loss and token reuse are part of the protocol</h2>
<p>The token is now correctness metadata. Treating a missing token as the default <code>g1</code> would allow a very old reader carrying <code>g1</code> to become valid again. This is the shape of an <strong>ABA problem</strong>: state changes from A to B and back to A, fooling a check that only compares the current value with the earlier one.</p>
<p>Instead, a fill must fail when the guard is absent. A read that finds no guard creates a fresh, never-reused token and deletes any surviving cached value, atomically, before proceeding. The read returns the token and cached value together. Thus losing the guard cannot turn an orphaned old value into a valid hit.</p>
<p>Do not give the guard the cached value’s short TTL. It may need to outlive a slow loader. But that alone is insufficient: eviction can remove it too. The missing-guard path is required, and the metadata itself adds memory and operational cost. The safe response to an unsuccessful initialisation is to skip this cache path, not to waive the token check.</p>
<p>Tokens must not repeat across initialisation or invalidation attempts. The diagram’s <code>g1</code> and <code>g2</code> are readable labels. A real implementation needs an appropriate unique-token generator and a retry policy that preserves the no-reuse rule. Generating a UUID once and letting a client replay the same old invalidation command later is not enough: it could restore a token superseded by another writer. Use a new token for every invalidation execution, including retries, and prevent automatic replay of an older rotation command.</p>
<p>The <a href="/learn/databases/caching/examples/invalidation.sh" download>invalidation worksheet</a> implements read/initialisation, rotation/deletion and conditional fill as short Redis scripts. It demonstrates the stale unguarded fill, rejection after rotation, a successful current fill, guard loss and safe reinitialisation. Save it and run <code>sh invalidation.sh</code> with Docker available. It creates and removes its own isolated Redis 8.2.2 container.</p>
<p>The Redis operations in that worksheet are real. The database price and the reader’s snapshot are held shell values, so the script tests a chosen schedule without pretending to test a database transaction. Its distinct literal tokens are for inspection, not a production token generator.</p>
<p>This protocol has boundaries. All cache readers and writers must use the protocol, all relevant source writes must trigger invalidation, loaders must read an appropriate source, and Redis must retain one ordered history of the guard operations. There is still a window between the source commit and invalidation. An already-running hit may also return after invalidation. A failover that restores an older guard and value can undo the boundary; Redis atomic execution is not a durability guarantee. We will return to that in the replication article.</p>
<p>Within the stated model, the claim is narrower and useful: once rotation has happened, a loader carrying the previous token cannot refill this cache. All ten interleavings of the model’s three reader steps and two writer steps were checked against that property.</p>

<h2>Make invalidation delivery recoverable</h2>
<p>So far, the writer always reached its invalidation step. A process can instead stop after committing £15 but before contacting Redis. The source has changed, but nothing has told the cache. Retrying the whole business operation may be inappropriate, and merely restarting the process does not recreate a notification it never recorded.</p>
<p>A <strong>transactional outbox</strong> records the source change and an event describing the required follow-up in the same source transaction. A separate worker delivers committed events and records progress. The transaction protects the obligation to invalidate; it does not make the later Redis operation simultaneous with the source commit.</p>
<InvalidationDelivery />
<p>With the outbox enabled, commit the change, crash the writer and restart it. The pending event remains available for delivery. Without it, the process loses the unsent obligation. Reads still hit £12 until another mechanism repairs the copy. Use Expire copy to see how TTL can provide that separate repair path.</p>
<p>Delivery can occur more than once if a worker completes the Redis operation but loses its acknowledgement. Consumers must tolerate retries. Replaying a plain deletion cannot install an old price, although it can discard a newer refill and cause extra work. After delivering once, read to refill £15, then replay the deletion to see that cost.</p>
<p>Generation rotation needs the extra no-reuse rule described above. A duplicate event is another request to invalidate; it must not restore the old generation token originally associated with that event.</p>
<p>Redis Pub/Sub alone does not provide this recovery queue. Its delivery is at most once: a disconnected subscriber can miss a message permanently. A durable event source, replay strategy and consumer progress are separate requirements. Change data capture can derive invalidations from committed database changes, but its lag, retention and restart behaviour still need to fit the application’s contract.</p>
<p>Operationally, watch the age of the oldest undelivered change, not only the number of events. A single stuck event can matter more than a large, fast-moving batch. Plan for delivery failure, duplicate processing and backlog recovery, and retain TTL or another repair mechanism appropriate to the data.</p>

<h2>Cover every dependency and every writer</h2>
<p>A product card can contain a category name, a price and a permission-dependent field. Invalidating only on changes to the product row leaves the other dependencies uncovered. A cached query can change when a previously absent row starts matching it. The dependency analysis from the keys and values article becomes the invalidation map.</p>
<p>Give each cached representation an owner and a concrete list of events that can make it unusable. Include administrative tools, imports and background jobs, not just the main application’s edit endpoint. An outbox in one writer does not cover another writer that updates the same source without emitting anything.</p>
<p>Sometimes dependency tracking costs more than the cache saves. Broader invalidation is easier to reason about but creates more misses. Smaller cached records reduce the set of changes each entry depends on, while leaving more assembly work on reads. A short, justified TTL can be an acceptable compromise for some displays. Authoritative reads may be the simplest correct answer for others.</p>
<p>Updating the cache with each write can avoid a subsequent miss, but it still needs ordering. If the source commits £15 and then £18, cache updates arriving in the opposite order can leave £15 behind. Source revisions and conditional updates can prevent that only when their ordering, loss and initialisation rules are defined. Replacing deletion with <code>SET</code> does not remove the coordination problem.</p>
<p>Likewise, deleting twice with a fixed sleep between deletions only catches loaders that finish within the chosen delay. A longer pause or a delayed message can move a stale fill beyond it. A timing heuristic should not be presented as a guarantee without a bound that makes it one.</p>

<h2>Test schedules, not just successful requests</h2>
<p>A cache can pass “write, then read” tests and still fail when those operations overlap. Place controllable barriers after the source read, after the source commit and before the cache fill. Hold an old loader while invalidation runs. Stop the writer before delivery. Remove guard metadata. Replay an old notification. Check both the current request’s answer and what a later request will find in the cache.</p>
<p>For the catalogue, we can now state a more useful design: source commits are authoritative; invalidation obligations survive writer-process failure; a generation check rejects fills from before a completed invalidation; a TTL provides additional repair; the editor’s own read and the purchase transaction use paths that meet their stronger requirements. Each clause has a failure case to test.</p>
<p>Making copies safe to discard exposes another issue. When a popular entry disappears, many callers may try to rebuild it at once. <a href="/learn/databases/caching/stampedes/">The next article examines cache stampedes</a>, where correctness alone does not keep the source alive.</p>

